# ── Optimizer (OpenRouter) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The optimizer route (src/app/api/optimize/route.ts) calls OpenRouter
# server-side, so the key never reaches the browser (no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix).
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY is required — it is the only place a key comes from, there
# is no in-app field. Without it the route returns 503 and the UI says the
# feature is not configured.
#
# OPENROUTER_MODEL is optional and overrides the default model slug
# (anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5). A model the client asks for must be in
# ALLOWED_MODELS (src/lib/optimizer/optimizer.ts), which lists only the cheap
# tier; this variable is not checked against that list, because it is set by
# whoever deploys the app. It is the way to run a bigger model for everyone.
#
# OPENROUTER_SITE_URL is optional: the URL sent to OpenRouter as the app's
# HTTP-Referer attribution. Unset, no Referer is sent.
#
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=anthropic/claude-haiku-4.5
# OPENROUTER_SITE_URL=https://your-app.example

# ── Spend controls ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIMIZER_DAILY_CALL_BUDGET caps upstream model calls per UTC day for the
# whole process (default 5000; a normal request is 3 calls, a deep one 7). When
# it is spent the route answers 503 until the next day. Per process, like the
# rate limiter — set a spend limit on the OpenRouter key as well.
#
# OPTIMIZER_DAILY_CALL_BUDGET=5000

# ── Pro detail ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The score breakdown, the issues list and the first-draft diff are only sent
# to the browser when the server says so — never merely hidden in the UI.
# There are no accounts yet, so it is one switch for the whole deployment:
# 1 = send it, 0 = withhold it. Unset means on outside production and off in
# production.
#
# OPTIMIZER_PRO=0

# ── Who is calling ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The route allows 20 optimizations per minute per caller (5 for Deep
# optimize), identified by IP. On Vercel it reads x-vercel-forwarded-for, which
# the platform sets and clients cannot forge; that header is IGNORED anywhere
# else, because off Vercel a client can send any value.
#
# Behind a different reverse proxy, set TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=1 to use
# x-forwarded-for / x-real-ip — but ONLY if that proxy overwrites the header
# rather than appending to a client-supplied one, or the limit is bypassable by
# anyone who sends the header themselves.
#
# In production with neither, the caller cannot be identified and the route
# answers 503 (it used to put everyone in one shared bucket, which let one
# client lock out the rest). Outside production a fixed key is used.
#
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=1

# ── Browser origins ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The route refuses cross-site browser requests: Origin must match the Host.
# List extra allowed origins here, comma-separated, if another site should be
# able to call it.
#
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://other.example
